@node Timely Internals
@section Internals

This section sketches how timely dataflow works underneath.  You do not
need any of it to write a program; it is here for the curious and for
anyone diagnosing a computation.

@subsection Communication

Underneath the dataflow layer is the communication layer, which starts
the worker threads, gives each an identity, and moves messages between
them.  A program selects the communication mode through the
@code{dtfl_config} (@pxref{Installation}): a single worker on the
calling thread, several worker threads in one process exchanging typed
queues, the same with serialized zero-copy byte queues, or many
processes exchanging over TCP.  Inter-worker data moves only through
these queues, and record batches are reference counted with atomic
counts because they cross threads (@pxref{Threads}).  You rarely touch
this layer directly; the point of the dataflow API is that you need not.

@subsection Progress tracking

Progress tracking is how multiple workers, moving data through a shared
dataflow graph, make meaningful statements about what data may still
arrive.  Each record bears a logical timestamp.  To send a message at a
time, an operator must hold a @dfn{capability} for that time
(@code{dtfl_cap_*}), so the set of outstanding capabilities at each
point bounds the times that can still appear there.

The mechanism has two halves: the workers collectively maintain a view
of the outstanding capabilities at each location in the graph, and each
worker independently computes and communicates the consequences of
changes in its own view.  Following each stream's @dfn{path summary}
(the least a time advances crossing an operator), the runtime propagates
those changes to produce every input's frontier.  When the set of
possible future times at a location becomes empty, that part of the
stream is complete.  A probe (@pxref{Running Timely Dataflows}) is a
read-only window onto this computed frontier.

@subsection Containers

Rust's timely is generic over the container that holds a batch of
records (a vector, a columnar layout, a shared reference).  libdtfl has
a single container: one arena of record bytes with an offsets array, and
for a differential batch, parallel time and diff columns.  Batches are
reference counted, so fan-out hands each consumer a reference rather
than a copy, and a consumer that mutates gets copy-on-write only when
the batch is shared.  The flat arena gives in C what the columnar layout
gives Rust, so there is no user-defined container type.

@subsection Logging

A worker logs structural and runtime events to named streams as it runs:
operator and channel creation, scheduling, messages, parking, and the
differential arrangement events.  Each event carries a timestamp of
elapsed time since the worker started.  These are the structured
machine-readable streams that the diagnostics tools consume
(@pxref{Diagnostics}); a program subscribes to a stream with
@code{dtfl_worker_log_subscribe} or @code{dtfl_config_log_subscribe} and
receives each batch of events already decoded.  This is distinct from
the human-readable diagnostic sink of @code{dtfl_set_log_hook}
(@pxref{Error handling}).
